{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788301090,"nonce":"KO3ljH+Uw2Mc6V3t5MHYiZGJHvg=","message":"BENCH PASS. Merged to main as `cb5de43`, rebuilt standalone in `db6656b`. Bench `_RimWorld-Agent` `20260901T213125`, `--quicktest`, assembly `1.0.0+cb5de43`. Envelopes and write-up at `accept/runs/s21-20260901/posture/`, commit `46dd291`.\n\n## The refutation was verified before the branch was touched\n\nThe worker reported that this issue's premise is backwards. I checked all four legs independently rather than taking it:\n\n1. `Data/Core/Defs/ThinkTreeDefs/Humanlike.xml` holds TWO defs — `Humanlike` and, at line 485, **`HumanlikeConstant`**, which is where `JobGiver_ConfigurableHostilityResponse` sits. `JobGiver_ReactToCloseMeleeThreat`, which reads the same field, is in the main tree.\n2. `HumanlikeConstant`'s root `subNodes` are `ThinkNode_Subtree(Despawned)`, `ThinkNode_ConditionalCanDoConstantThinkTreeJobNow`, `ThinkNode_ConditionalCanDoLordJobNow` — **none of `ThinkTreeInjector`'s four anchors** (`ThinkNode_ConditionalColonist`, `ThinkNode_QueuedJob`, `ThinkNode_Subtree(LordDuty)`, `ThinkNode_ConditionalRevenantState`). Note the third: that tree's Lord subtree is `LordDutyConstant`, not `LordDuty`, so the anchor genuinely misses. `Inject()` hits `continue`.\n3. `Verse.AI/Pawn_JobTracker.DetermineNextJob` calls `DetermineNextConstantThinkTreeJob()` FIRST and returns on a hit.\n4. Op 109 of the M1 run — seek ON, `hostility_response: Flee`, Captain in `JobDriver_FleeAndCower` — is impossible under the old reading and is what the new one predicts.\n\n**So `Flee` beats seek and `hostility:\"Attack\"` is load-bearing, not a backstop.** The body of this issue and the lesson both had it the other way.\n\n## The first fix was also wrong, and that is worth recording\n\nThe worker's initial `flee` verdict cited \"\u003c= 8 cells with line of sight (`ShouldFleeFrom`, `checkDistance:true`)\". Those are the ATTACK branch's numbers. Sent back; the corrected account is better than either version and I verified it too:\n\n- **TRIGGER** — `TryGetFleeJob` opens on `SelfDefenseUtility.ShouldStartFleeing`, the only place distance and sight are tested: `ShouldFleeFrom(checkDistance:true, checkLOS:false)` over `AlwaysFlee`, then `(checkDistance:true, checkLOS:true)` over `AttackTarget` in a 9-region `BreadthFirstTraverse`. `checkDistance:true` is literally `t.Position.InHorDistOf(pawn.Position, 8f)` in `FleeUtility.ShouldFleeFrom`.\n- **DESTINATION** — once triggered, it re-gathers with `checkDistance:false, checkLOS:false` and hands every threat the caches hold to `CellFinderLoose.GetFleeDest`.\n\n**That gap is M1's 150 cells.** One crow inside eight cells starts the flee; the whole map chooses where the pawn runs to. Neither the old lesson nor the first fix said that.\n\n## Acceptance on a bench\n\n**`digest.posture` before anything was set:**\n\n    ok: false   will_seek 0/1   area_bound 0/3   attack 0/1\n    on_contact: {flee: 3, everything else 0}\n    flee_risk: [{name: \"Teresa\", pawn: 317}]\n\n`flee: 3` — all three colonists would run on contact, in one field, at every read. The M1 state, said out loud.\n\n**One call, `levers: [\"area\",\"seek\",\"hostility\"]`, `accepted 3 / rejected 0`:**\n\n| pawn | violence | area | hostility | seek | `on_contact` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 317 Teresa | capable | Area 2, 196 cells | **Attack** | **on** | **attack-then-seek** |\n| 310 Danielle | incapable | Area 2, 196 cells | Flee | off | flee |\n| 314 Greve | incapable | Area 2, 196 cells | Flee | off | flee |\n\n**`digest.posture` after:** `ok:true`, `will_seek 1/1`, `area_bound 3/3`, `attack 1/1`, `on_contact {flee: 2, attack-then-seek: 1}`, `flee_risk: []`.\n\n`ok:true` with two pawns still fleeing is CORRECT, and the block states its own denominators: `will_seek` and `attack` are over violence-capable free colonists, `area_bound` over those whose `Pawn_PlayerSettings.SupportsAllowedAreas` is true. `1/1` means every pawn who can, does.\n\n**\"A pawn incapable of violence is refused BY NAME\" — met, and the fixture was free.** `ff1f0b9` reports that a violence-incapable pawn cannot be forced by any shipped verb. True, but this `--quicktest` map ROLLED TWO out of three, so the bullet is provable opportunistically. And the verb does better than refuse: `incapable_of_violence` names each with the game's own reason and adds \"The area still binds; this pawn will never fight\" — which a flat refusal would have hidden, since two of the three settings do apply to them.\n\n**The refusal that makes this one verb:** `posture {seek:true}` with no `area` is `bad-args` — \"a posture with two of them is the bug this verb exists to remove … or `area:null` to DECLARE unrestricted deliberately.\" You cannot set two of three by accident, and unrestricted is a declaration rather than an omission.\n\n## Owed\n\n**The save/load round trip**, the one bullet needing a bench restart. Not run.","files":null}]}